\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Open_Watcom_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Open Watcom Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "Contents()") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0001}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Open Watcom Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Imagine being assigned to a project with thousands of lines of C++ source code.  Learning the relationship between the objects of such an application can be very difficult.  The Browser was developed to solve this very problem - to help developers better understand the source code they are working with.  Information such as the class inheritance hierarchy and the call tree for an application can be studied while using the Browser.  Once you have located a symbol, you can quickly view the source file that contains its definition or list all the files that reference it.  Viewing these relationships helps you understand how the program works.
\par 
\par This chapter describes the many tasks you can perform with the Browser, including creating and opening a Browser database file, configuring the Browser session, and viewing the list of all symbols in your program, the class inheritance hierarchy, and call structure.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Using_the_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Using the Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0002}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Using the Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par This section discusses the following topics:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Starting the Browser
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Starting the Browser through the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Quitting the Browser
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The Browser Menu Bar
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Starting_the_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Starting the Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Using_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;start, Browser;Browser, start}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0003}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Starting the Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par To start the Browser, double click on the Browser icon.  This opens the Browser window.  The caption bar of this window displays the current option file and Browser database file.  The message\f4 \fs24  no browser file\f2 \fs20  appears on this line if a database file is not currently selected.
\par 
\par The Browser is an integrated tool that you can open from the IDE so that you can browse the source code of the project you are working on.  Refer to the IDE guide for further information on the Integrated Development Environment.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Quitting_the_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Quitting the Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Using_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;leave, Browser;Browser, leave}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0004}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Quitting the Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par To exit the Browser, choose\b  Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu of the Browser window.  If you made changes to the options during the Browser session, a message box appears prompting you to save the changes.
\par 
\par Choose\b  No\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the message box to close the Browser session without saving.  Any options changed within the session are lost.
\par 
\par Choose\b  Yes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the message box to save the current options to the current option file, if one exists, and exit the Browser session.  If no option file exists, a\b  Save As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog appears that allows you to specify the option file to which you want to save the new options.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Cancel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to return to the main Browser window without saving the options.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog and exit the Browser session.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Browser_Menu_Bar}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Browser Menu Bar}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Using_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, Menu Bar}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0005}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Browser Menu Bar \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The Browser's menu bar consists of the following eight menus:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b File\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Create, open, and configure Browser files; save and load options
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b View\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose a global view for the current database file
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Detail\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 View detailed information for a symbol
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Tree\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose the nodes to display for a tree view
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Locate\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Find symbols within the current global view
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Options\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Configure the Browser session
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Windows\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Select from the list of Browser windows currently open
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Help\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Access on-line help information
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Browser_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Browser Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;module file, in Browser;Browser, files}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0006}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Browser Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par To browse your source code, you must first create a Browser module file for each source file that you wish to browse.  Currently, browsing is supported by the Open Watcom C/C++ compilers only (Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 does not support browsing).  To create a Browser module file, specify the "db" option when you compile the source file.  If you are using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, select\b  Emit Browser Information\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Debugging Switches\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  panel of the\b  Compiler Switches\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  The Browser module file will have the same name as the source file and an extension of ".MBR".
\par 
\par Once the Browser module files have been created, these files are merged and a Browser database file is created.  This process eliminates redundant information such as duplicate definitions that occur when a header file is included by many source files.  Browser database files have the extension ".DBR".
\par 
\par The Browser allows you to configure your session and saves this configuration to an options file.  Options files have the extension ".OBR".  The file name\f4 \fs24  setup.obr\f2 \fs20  is the default name of the options file.  The default options file is automatically loaded by the Browser when the Browser is started.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Creating_a_Browser_Database_File_within_the_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Creating a Browser Database File within the Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Browser_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;database file, create in Browser;Browser, create database file}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0007}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Creating a Browser Database File within the Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  New\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  New Browser File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you enter the name of the Browser database file you are creating.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow1.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 1.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the New Browser File dialog to create a new Browser database file.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Type the name and path\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the new Browser database file.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Click on the\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button to open a second dialog that allows you to browse the directory structure for an existing Browser database file.  Select an existing file and change its name.  If you do not change its name, a message box appears when you press\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  asking if you want to overwrite the existing file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par This opens a\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window that will eventually contain a list of the module files that will make up the database file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Add\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Select Module File(s)\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you choose the module files to add to the database file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select the module files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to add to the database file and click on\b  OK.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Double click\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the desired module file.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Select Module File(s)\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and adds the selected module file to the Browser file component list.  Each module file on the components list has a check box.  When added, this check box is marked with an X, indicating that the module file is enabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (6)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Continue with steps 4 and 5\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have added all desired module files to the component list.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow2.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 2.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Modules dialog displays the selected module files.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (7)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window and creates the database file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Creating_a_Browser_Database_File_from_the_Command_Line}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Creating a Browser Database File from the Command Line}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Browser_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;quiet option;merger, quiet option;CBR files;DBR files;MBR files;merger, with Browser;Browser, merger utility;database file, create from command line;Browser, create database file from command line}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0008}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Creating a Browser Database File from the Command Line \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par It is also possible to create the Browser database file from the command line.  This allows you to make Browser database file creation part of your standard build procedure.  When you do this, the batch build procedure will automatically update your database file.  Updating the Browser database file occurs only if changes have been made to a module file within the database file.
\par 
\par A separate utility, called the merger, is used to create the database file.  The name of the merger program is\f4 \fs24  wbrg.exe.\f2 \fs20   Its command line consists of the name of the database file and a list of the module files (.MBR files) to be merged.  The name of the database file must be preceded by a\f4 \fs24  database\f2 \fs20  command.  The default extension given to the database file is ".DBR".  The list of module files must be preceded by a\f4 \fs24  file\f2 \fs20  command.  The module file names must be separated by commas or enclosed by curly braces and separated by spaces.  The list of module file names can contain wild cards.  The following are examples of valid merger commands.  In each case, the module files\f4 \fs24  m1.mbr\f2 \fs20  and\f4 \fs24  m2.mbr\f2 \fs20  will be processed and the database file\f4 \fs24  db.dbr\f2 \fs20  will be created.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      wbrg database db file m1, m2\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      wbrg database db file \{ m1 m2 \}\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      wbrg file m1, m2 database db\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      wbrg file \{ m1 m2 \} database db\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par It is also possible to specify a command file that contains merger commands.  Command files have the extension ".CBR".  Consider a command file, called\f4 \fs24  merge.cbr,\f2 \fs20  containing the following merger commands.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      database db\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file m1\f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file m2\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par The following example will achieve the same results as the previous example.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      wbrg @merge\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par If you want to suppress the listing of file names that the merger produces as it is working, you can include the "quiet" option on the command line or in the command file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Opening_an_Existing_Browser_Database_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Opening an Existing Browser Database File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Browser_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;open, database file in Browser;database file, open in Browser;Browser, open database file}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0009}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Opening an Existing Browser Database File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Open\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Open Browser Database File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you select the database file you want to open.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select a database file to open and click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Double click\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the desired database file.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Open Browser Database File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 You can have only one database file open at a time.  If you open a second database file, the Browser discards the first and displays information for the second.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Working_with_Browser_Module_Files}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Working with Browser Module Files}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Browser_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, working with module files;module file, in Browser}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0010}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Working with Browser Module Files \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once a Browser database file is loaded, you might want to edit the list of modules originally used to create the database file.  Editing the list of modules allows you to temporarily remove modules from the database or add modules to the database.  For example, you may decide that you only want to browse a particular module or set of modules or you may have forgotten a module when the database was originally created.
\par 
\par The\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu item in the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to view the list of module files that make up the current database file.  From this list you can perform the following functions on the module files:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Add
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Remove
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Disable
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Enable
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow3.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 3.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  On the Modules dialog, you can add, remove, disable, and enable module files.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Adding_a_Module_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Adding a Module File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Working_with_Browser_Module_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Module file, add in Browser;Browser, add Module file}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0011}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Adding a Module File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog for the current database file.  This dialog lists all module files that make up the database file.  Each module file has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the module file is enabled.  The Browser browses only the enabled module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Add\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Select Module File(s)\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you choose the module files to add to the current Browser file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select the module files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to add to the Browser file and click on\b  OK.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   Select multiple module files by holding the Shift key while you click on the desired files.
\par 
\par Clicking on OK closes the\b  Select Module File(s)\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and adds the selected module file to the database file.  Each module file in the module list has a check box.  When added, this check box is marked with an X, indicating that the module file is enabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Continue with steps 2 and 3\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have added all desired module files to the component list.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and updates the database file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Removing_a_Module_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Removing a Module File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Working_with_Browser_Module_Files')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0012}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Removing a Module File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog for the current database file.  This dialog lists all module files that make up the database file.  Each module file has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the module file is enabled.  The Browser browses only the enabled module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the module file\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  you want to remove from the database file.
\par 
\par This highlights the selected module file.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Remove\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button in the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This removes the selected module file from the list.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and updates the database file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Disabling_a_Module_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Disabling a Module File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Working_with_Browser_Module_Files')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Module file, disable in Browser;Browser, disable module file}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0013}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Disabling a Module File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog for the current database file.  This dialog lists all module files that make up the database file.  Each module file has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the module file is enabled.  The Browser browses only the enabled module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click in the check box\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the module file you want to disable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the module file you wish to disable.  Press the space bar to disable the currently selected module file.
\par 
\par This removes the X.  The blank box indicates that the module file is disabled and will not be browsed.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Repeat step 2\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have disabled all desired module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and updates the database file.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par To disable all of the module files in the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog, click on the\b  Disable All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.  Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to update the database file and close the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Enabling_a_Module_File}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Enabling a Module File}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Working_with_Browser_Module_Files')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0014}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Enabling a Module File \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog for the current database file.  This dialog lists all module files that make up the database file.  Each module file has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the module file is enabled.  The Browser browses only the enabled module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click in the check box\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the module file you want to enable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the module file you wish to enable.  Press the space bar to enable the currently selected module file.
\par 
\par This places an X in the box indicating that the module file is enabled and will be browsed.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Repeat step 2\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have enabled all desired module files.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  on the\b  Module\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par This closes the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog and updates the database file.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par To enable all of the module files on the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window, click on the\b  Enable All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.  Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to update the database file and close the\b  Modules\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Global_Views}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Global Views}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0015}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Global Views \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The menu items under the View menu let you display a global view of your program.  A global view is one that displays relationships between all symbols in your program.  The following are global views.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b List\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Displays a list of all symbols in your program
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Inheritance\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Displays the class inheritance graph for your program
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Call\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Displays the call graph for your program
\par \li0\fi0 
\par Once a global view has been displayed, you can view detailed information for the symbols in the global view.  Refer to the section entitled {\f2\uldb Viewing\~Detail\~Information\v\f2\uldb Viewing_Detail_Information\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for a discussion on displaying detail information.
\par 
\par It is possible to specify a query that restricts the symbols displayed in global views.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Global\~Symbol\~Queries\v\f2\uldb Global_Symbol_Queries\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_List_View}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The List View}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;symbols, browsing;Browser, browsing symbols}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0016}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The List View \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Using the Browser to view the symbols in your program is much faster than searching through your source code for symbol information.  From the symbols list you can quickly access detailed information on a symbol that tells you where the symbol is used and where it is defined.
\par 
\par To view a list of all symbols in the current Browser database file, choose\b  List\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  View\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  This displays a window which can list all symbols in your program.  The window has a vertical scroll bar that allows you to scroll through the list of symbols in your program.  Since your program may contain a very large number of symbols, the Browser does not load all the symbols in your program from the database.  Instead, only the number of symbols that can be displayed in the window are loaded.  As far as the Browser is concerned, this list is infinite.  For this reason, the scroll thumb on the vertical scroll bar is positioned in the middle of the vertical scroll bar and cannot be moved.  Click below the vertical scroll thumb to view the next page of symbols and above the vertical scroll thumb to view the previous page of symbols.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow4.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 4.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The List window displays all symbols in the current browser database file.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par Each symbol has a icon to its left.  A letter marking each icon indicates the symbol type in the source code as follows:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b F\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Function
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b C\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Class
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b T\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Typedef
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b V\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Variable
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b E\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Enum
\par \li0\fi0 
\par There are several ways to display the detail view of symbols.  When you reveal the detail view, the file folder icon changes to an open file folder.  To close the detail view dialog, click on the file folder.  This changes the icon back to a closed folder and closes the dialog.  To reveal the detail view of a symbol you can perform any of the following actions:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab The file folder icons are hot spots that display the detail view of the selected symbol.  To activate the hot spot, click once on the symbol name to select it and press ENTER to reveal the detail view.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the file folder to reveal the detail view for that symbol.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the symbol name to select it and choose\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Double click on the symbol name.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Class_Inheritance_View}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Class Inheritance View}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;inheritance menu item;browsing classes;Browser, browsing classes}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0017}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Class Inheritance View \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Selecting\b  Inheritance\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  View\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu displays the inheritance hierarchy of all of the C++ classes in your program using a tree.  This allows you to see the relationships between base classes and derived classes.  In the inheritance hierarchy, each node represents a class.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow5.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 5.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Inheritance view displays the hierarchy of all C++ classes in your program (graph view shown).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par There are two different tree views you can choose from to display the class hierarchy:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Graph view\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The graph view displays each class in a box.  A box, or node, is connected to another node if one is a derived class of the other.  The root node of the tree is the base class for all nodes in the tree.  The tree is initially fully expanded.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Manipulating\~the\~Tree\~Views\v\f2\uldb Manipulating_the_Tree_Views\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Outline view\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The outline view displays the same information as the graph view but in a different way.  The outline view is initially fully collapsed.  That is, only the base classes are displayed.  In order to view the derived classes of a node, you must single click on the node.  This expands the node, displaying all its immediate derived classes.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Manipulating\~the\~Tree\~Views\v\f2\uldb Manipulating_the_Tree_Views\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # The_Function_Call_Tree_View}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ The Function Call Tree View}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;call menu item;browsing functions;Browser, browsing functions}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0018}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 The Function Call Tree View \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Selecting\b  Call\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  View\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu displays the function call tree for all functions in your program.  This allows you to see all the functions that a given function calls and conversely all functions that call a certain function.  In the call tree, each node represents a function.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow6.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 6.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Call view displays a call tree for all functions in your program (outline view shown).\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par There are two different tree views you can choose from to display the function call tree:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Graph view\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The graph view displays each function in a box.  A box, or node, is connected to another node if one function calls the other.  The tree is initially fully expanded.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Manipulating\~the\~Tree\~Views\v\f2\uldb Manipulating_the_Tree_Views\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Outline view\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The outline view displays the same information as the graph view but in a different way.  The outline view is initially fully collapsed.  In order to view the functions called by the function specified in the node, you must single click on the node.  This expands the node, displaying all functions it calls.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Manipulating\~the\~Tree\~Views\v\f2\uldb Manipulating_the_Tree_Views\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Manipulating_the_Tree_Views}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Manipulating the Tree Views}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, outline view;Browser, graph view}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0019}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Manipulating the Tree Views \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par With both the graph view and the outline view you can change the information displayed in the following ways:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab select the root nodes you want to view
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab expand any node in the view
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab collapse any node in the view
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par You expand and collapse nodes in the graph and outline views to hide and reveal descendant and ancestor nodes of the selected node.  There are two ways to expand and collapse nodes:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab choose the desired function from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab click on the node (applies only to the outline view)
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par In addition to these functions, you can force the graph to be redrawn when a node in the tree view is collapsed.  By default, collapsed nodes will leave a gap in the graph.  Redrawing the graph removes these gaps.  This feature is controlled by an option.  See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Configuring\~the\~Browser\v\f2\uldb Configuring_the_Browser\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information.
\par 
\par Clicking on the right mouse button when the mouse cursor is in a tree view will automatically display the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  This allows you to perform the actions in the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu without actually going to the menu bar.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Selecting_Root_Nodes}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Selecting Root Nodes}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Manipulating_the_Tree_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Root Nodes, enable;Browser, enable root nodes;Browser, disable root nodes;Root Nodes, disable;Browser, selecting root nodes;Nodes, root;Root Nodes}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0020}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Selecting Root Nodes \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Select\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to change the root nodes that are displayed.  The root nodes you select appear in the graph or outline view; all other root nodes are hidden.
\par 
\par Choosing\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu opens the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog lists all of the symbols that appear as a root node.  Each symbol in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog has a check box.  When enabled, this check box is marked with an X, indicating that the symbol will appear as a root node in the display.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow7.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 7.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  On the Select Root Nodes dialog, choose the symbols you want to appear as root nodes.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2 \fs24 \b Disabling Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click in the check box\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the root node you want to disable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the root node you wish to disable.  Press the space bar to disable the currently selected root node.
\par 
\par The X disappears meaning that the root node is disabled and will not appear in the display.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Repeat step one\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have disabled all desired root nodes.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par The\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog closes and the display is updated.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par To disable all of the root nodes in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog, click on the\b  Disable All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.  This removes Xs from each box in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog and update the display.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 This disables the selected root nodes only for the active view window.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f2 \fs24 \b Enabling Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click in the check box\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the root node you want to enable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the root node you wish to enable.  Press the space bar to enable the currently selected root node.
\par 
\par An X appears in the box indicating that the root node is enabled and will appear in the display.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Repeat step one\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  until you have enabled all desired root nodes.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.
\par 
\par The\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog closes and the display is updated.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par To enable all of the root nodes in the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog, click on the\b  Enable All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.  An X appears in each box on the\b  Select Root Nodes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog and update the display.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 This enables the selected root nodes only for the active view window.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Expanding_Nodes}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Expanding Nodes}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Manipulating_the_Tree_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, expanding nodes;Nodes, expanding}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0021}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Expanding Nodes \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can expand the display all at once, one level at a time, or one branch at a time.  To expand by levels and branches, you must first select the node you want to expand.  Do this by clicking once on the desired node.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Expand One Level\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose\b  Expand One Level\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to display all of the immediate children for the selected node.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Expand Branch\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose\b  Expand Branch\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to display all descendants of the selected node.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Expand All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose\b  Expand All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to fully expand all of the enabled root nodes.  Disabled root nodes do not appear in the display.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Collapsing_Nodes}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Collapsing Nodes}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Manipulating_the_Tree_Views')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;using the keyboard, to expand and collapse nodes;expanding and collapsing nodes, using the keyboard;Browser, collapsing nodes;Nodes, collapsing}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0022}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Collapsing Nodes \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can collapse the display all at once or one branch at a time.  To collapse by branches, you must first select the node you want to collapse.  Do this by clicking once on the desired node.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Collapse Branch\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose\b  Collapse Branch\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to hide all descendants of the selected node.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Collapse All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Choose\b  Collapse All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to collapse all of the nodes and display only the root nodes.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f2 \fs24 \b Using the Keyboard\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f2 \fs20 
\par You can expand and collapse the symbols using key sequences.  Click once on the symbol name to highlight it and press one of the following keys:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b +\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Expand the symbol one level
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b -\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Collapse the symbol one level
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b *\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Expand all descendants of the selected node.  This is the same as choosing\b  Expand Branch\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b CTRL *\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Expands all of the enabled root nodes.  This is the same as choosing\b  Expand All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b CTRL -\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Collapses all of the symbols and displays only the root nodes.  This is the same as choosing\b  Collapse All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Viewing_Detail_Information}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Viewing Detail Information}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, view detail information}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0023}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Viewing Detail Information \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once a global view is displayed, you can view detailed information for a symbol in the global view in several ways:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Double click on the desired symbol.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the desired symbol to select it; then choose\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the desired symbol to select it then press ENTER.
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par From the list view, you can reveal the detail view if you:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the icon to the left of the symbol.
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par Performing any of these actions reveals a detail view window for the selected symbol.  The same information appears in this window regardless of the method used to access it.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow8.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 8.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The detail view displays detailed information for the selected symbol.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The detail view window displays the source file where the symbol is defined or declared and it shows you what the symbol looks like in your source code.
\par 
\par A list box appears in the detail view window when you select a symbol whose type is a function, class, or enum.  The list box contains information specific to the symbol type, as follows.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Functions\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The list box displays, where applicable, local variables for the function.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Classes\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The list box displays member variables and member functions for the selected class and any inherited classes.  The Browser divides this information into three categories:
\par 
\par \li720 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Public
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Private
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Protected
\par \fi0 \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Enums\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The list box displays enumerator values for the selected enumerator.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par You can view detailed information for symbols in the list box by double-clicking on the symbol.  Other symbols in the header information for the detail view are highlighted.  Double-clicking on these symbols also displays a detail view.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Goto_Definition}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Goto Definition}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Viewing_Detail_Information')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Goto Definition function;Browser, Goto Definition function}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0024}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Goto Definition \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par From a detail view,\b  Goto Definition\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to edit the file that contains the symbol's definition.  The editor is positioned on the line and column containing the symbols definition.  This allows you to make edits to your source code while you are browsing.  When you are done, save any changes made and exit the editor to return to the Browser session.
\par 
\par Note that any changes to your source code will make the Browser database out-of-date.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Source_References}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Source References}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Viewing_Detail_Information')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Source References;Browser, source references}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0025}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Source References \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Selecting\b  Source References\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu displays all locations in the source code where a symbol is referenced.  This allows you to analyze all uses of a particular symbol.
\par 
\par When modifying a symbol, you can use this feature to locate all occurrences of the symbol in the source code so you can update them.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To view the source references for a symbol:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Position yourself at the detail view of the symbol\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose source references you want to view.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Source References\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Source References\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window for the selected symbol.  This window displays the filename, line number, and column number of each occurrence of the selected symbol in the source code.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow9.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 9.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Source References window to view all the occurrences of the selected symbol in the source code.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Symbol_References}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Symbol References}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Viewing_Detail_Information')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, symbol references;Symbol references}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0026}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Symbol References \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \b  Symbol References\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  allows you to view a list of all symbols that use a particular symbol.  When modifying a symbol you can use this feature to locate all symbols using the modified symbol.  This allows you to determine if all referencing symbols need to be updated.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To view the symbol references for a symbol:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Position yourself at the detail view of the symbol\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  whose symbol references you want to view.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Symbol References\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Detail\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Symbol Referencing\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window for the selected symbol.  This window displays a list of all symbols that use the selected symbol.  The display is the same as the list view of symbols.  Each symbol has a icon indicating its symbol type.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow10.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 10.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Symbols window to view a list of all symbols that use the selected symbol.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par You can perform the same actions in this window as from the\b  List\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  window.  Refer to the section entitled {\f2\uldb The\~List\~View\v\f2\uldb The_List_View\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par From this window you can invoke the editor to make changes to the source code that contains the symbols that reference a particular symbol.  Select the reference you want to edit in one of two ways:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Double click on the desired reference.
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Click once on the desired reference to select it and press ENTER.
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par This invokes the editor and positions the cursor at the line that contains the selected reference.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Locating_Symbols}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Locating Symbols}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, locating symbols;Symbols, locating}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0027}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Locating Symbols \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When a global view is displayed and your program contains many symbols, it can be difficult to scroll through the global view to locate a particular symbol.  There are two functions that can help you locate a symbol in a global view:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Find
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Find Selected
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Find}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Find}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Locating_Symbols')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0028}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Find \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Choose\b  Find\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Locate\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to open the\b  Find\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Use this dialog to specify the criteria to be used to locate a symbol.  For example, you can search for a particular symbol or a set of symbols with common characteristics.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow11.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 11.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Find dialog to locate one symbol or a set of symbols.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Find_Pattern}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Find Pattern}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Find')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;find pattern;Browser, find pattern}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0029}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Find Pattern \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field of the\b  Find\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog, enter a pattern for the symbol(s) you wish to locate.  The matching behaviour depends on the switches set in this dialog.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Match Case\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser performs a case sensitive compare when attempting to find a match.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Match Whole Symbols Only\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser locates symbols that exactly match the specified pattern.  This switch only applies when the\b  Use Regular Expressions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  switch is disabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Use Regular Expressions\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser interprets the pattern specified as a regular expression.  The\b  Edit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, when pressed, displays the\b  Regular Expression Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the regular expression.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par Dropping the combo box of the\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field displays a list of previous patterns entered.  You can select a pattern from this list instead of entering a new one.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Find_Filters}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Find Filters}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Find')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, find filters;find filters}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0030}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Find Filters \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Clicking on the\b  Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button on the\b  Find\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog opens the\b  Find Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the characteristics of the symbol(s) to be located.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow12.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 12.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Find Filters dialog to specify characteristics of the symbol(s) you want to locate.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par In the\b  Symbol Type\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog click on the symbol types you want to view.  To search for all symbol types, select the\b  All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par 
\par In the\b  Symbol Scope\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section, you can specify the scope for locating symbols.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Member of Class:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Specify the class that the symbol must be a member of in order to be located.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Local Symbols of Function:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Specify the function that the symbol must be local to in order to be located.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \f2 \fs24 \b Find File Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f2 \fs20 
\par The\b  Source Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, when pressed, displays the\b  Source Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog that allows you to specify the files to be searched when attempting to locate a symbol.  This dialog lists all source files that make up the database file.  Each source file in the list has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the source file is searched when trying to locate a symbol.  Click in the check box of the source file you want to disable.  This removes the X.  The blank box indicates that the source file will not be searched when trying to locate a symbol.  To re-enable the source file, click in the check box again.  This places an X in the box indicating that the source file will be searched.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow13.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 13.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Choose the source files to be searched using the Source Files dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par The\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog, allows you to specify wild card directory specifications for files that are to be included or excluded in the search.  For example, specifying "d:\\watcom\\h\\*.h" and pressing the\b  Exclude\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, will prevent any file in the "d:\\watcom\\h" directory with extension ".h" from being searched.  To include these files in the search again, specify the same pattern and press the\b  Include\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par 
\par Pressing the\b  Set All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button includes all files in the search.
\par 
\par Pressing the\b  Clear All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button excludes all files in the search.  This is useful, for example, if you wish to only search files in the current directory.  Simply press the\b  Clear All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, specify "*.*" in the Pattern section, and press the\b  Include\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Performing_the_Find}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Performing the Find}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Find')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0031}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Performing the Find \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Once the filter criteria are set, perform the find operation by clicking on the\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button on the\b  Find\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This closes the dialog and performs the search.  The Browser examines the symbols in the current global view and selects the first symbol that matches the search criteria.
\par 
\par To find the next symbol that matches the search criteria, choose\b  Find Next\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Locate\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Find_Selected}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Find Selected}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Locating_Symbols')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, find selected}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0032}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Find Selected \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Because the symbols displayed in a global view are often many, only a portion of the display is visible in the window at any time.  When you temporarily scroll away from the selected symbol or node, a quick method of locating that symbol is to select\b  Find Selected\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Locate\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  This will immediately locate and display the currently selected symbol in the global view.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Configuring_the_Browser}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Configuring the Browser}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, configure}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0033}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Configuring the Browser \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are several options in the Browser that you can configure:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Regular expression processing for find and query
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Global Symbol Queries
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Enumerator styles for detail views of enumeration constants
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Member filters for detail views of classes
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Auto-arranging of graph views
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Line drawing method for graph views
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Orientation of graph views
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Line styles and colors for inheritance graphs
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Line styles and colors for call graphs
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Selection of text editor
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Automatic saving of options on exit
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par This section describes each configuration option.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Regular_Expressions_for_Find_and_Query}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Regular Expressions for Find and Query}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;regular expressions, in Browser;Browser, set regular expressions for queries;Browser, set regular expressions for find}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0034}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Regular Expressions for Find and Query \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \b  Regular Expressions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to configure the regular expression used to find a symbol and specify a query.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow14.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 14.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Regular Expressions Options dialog to configure a regular expression for Find and Query.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par Choose\b  Regular Expressions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to open the\b  Regular Expressions Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  In the\b  Search String Meaning\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog you select the anchoring method used to find a match.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Starts With\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Matches only if the pattern is found at the beginning of the symbol
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Contains\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Matches if it occurs anywhere in the symbol
\par \li0\fi0 
\par In the\b  Regular Expression Characters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog you select the characters you want the Browser to interpret as meta-characters.
\par 
\par Select the desired characters by clicking once in the corresponding check box.  An X in the check box indicates that the character will be interpreted as a meta-character.  Unchecked characters are matched as standard keyboard characters.  To de-select a character, click again on its check box.
\par 
\par The other buttons on this dialog are:
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Set All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Click on Set All to enable all of the characters.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Clear All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Click on Clear All to disable all of the characters.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Defaults\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Click on Defaults to discard the current settings in this dialog and replace them with the settings configured at the start of the Browser session.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b OK\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close this dialog.  This changes the configuration for the current Browser session.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Global_Symbol_Queries}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Global Symbol Queries}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0035}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Global Symbol Queries \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Choose\b  Query\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to open the\b  Query\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Use this dialog to specify the criteria to be used to display symbols in a global view.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow15.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 15.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Query dialog to configure global view symbol queries.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Query_Pattern}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Query Pattern}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Symbol_Queries')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;query pattern;Browser, specifying the query pattern}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0036}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Query Pattern \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field of the\b  Query\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog, enter a pattern for the symbol(s) you wish to display in the global views.  The matching behaviour depends on the switches set in this dialog.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Match Case\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser performs a case sensitive compare when attempting to find a match.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Match Whole Symbols Only\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser includes symbols that exactly match the specified pattern.  This switch only applies when the\b  Use Regular Expressions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  switch is disabled.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Use Regular Expressions\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 When enabled, the Browser interprets the pattern specified as a regular expression.  The\b  Edit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button is enables and, when pressed, displays the\b  Regular Expression Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the regular expression.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par Dropping the combo box of the\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field displays a list of previous patterns entered.  You can select a pattern from this list instead of entering a new one.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Query_Filters}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Query Filters}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Global_Symbol_Queries')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, query filters;query filters}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0037}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Query Filters \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Clicking on the\b  Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button on the\b  Query\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog opens the\b  Query Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the characteristics of the symbol(s) to be displayed in the global views.
\par 
\par In the\b  Symbol Type\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog click on the symbol types you want to view.  To display symbols of all types, click on the\b  All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par 
\par In the\b  Symbol Scope\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section, you can specify the scope of the symbols to be displayed.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Member of Class:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Specify the class that the symbol must be a member of in order to be displayed.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Local Symbols of Function:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Specify the function that the symbol must be local to in order to be displayed.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par In the\b  Symbol Attributes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section, you can specify the attributes of the symbols to be displayed.  Selecting\b  Artificial\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  will cause compiler-generated symbols to be displayed.  Selecting\b  Anonymous\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  will cause unnamed types to be displayed.  Unnamed types will be displayed as square brackets enclosing the symbols whose type they define.  Selecting\b  Declared Only\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  will cause only symbols that have been defined to be displayed.  For example, a function prototype for an unreferenced function will not be displayed when\b  Declared Only\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  is selected.
\par 
\par \f2 \fs24 \b Query File Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \f2 \fs20 
\par The\b  Source Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, when pressed, displays the\b  Source Files\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog that allows you to specify the files that a symbol must be defined in in order to be displayed.  This dialog lists all source files that make up the database file.  Each source file in the list has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that all symbols defined in the source file will be displayed.  Click in the check box of the source file you want to disable.  This removes the X.  The blank box indicates that any symbols defined in the source file will not be displayed.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the source file you wish to disable.  Press the space bar to disable the currently selected source file.  To re-enable the source file, click in the check box again or press the space bar.  This places an X in the box again.
\par 
\par The\b  Pattern\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog, allows you to specify wild card directory specifications for files.  Any symbols defined in these files will be displayed in the global views.  For example, specifying\f4 \fs24  d:\\watcom\\h\\*.h\f2 \fs20  and pressing the\b  Exclude\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, will prevent any symbol defined in any file in the\f4 \fs24  d:\\watcom\\h\f2 \fs20  directory with extension ".h" from being displayed.  To display symbols from these files again, specify the same pattern and press the\b  Include\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par 
\par Pressing the\b  Set All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button displays all symbols in all files.
\par 
\par Pressing the\b  Clear All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button causes no symbols to be displayed.  This is useful, for example, if you wish to only display symbols defined in files in the current directory.  Simply press the\b  Clear All\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button, specify\f4 \fs24  *.*\f2 \fs20  in the Pattern section, and press the\b  Include\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  button.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Enumerator_Styles}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Enumerator Styles}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, enumeration styles;enumeration styles}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0038}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Enumerator Styles \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Selecting\b  Enumeration Styles\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu displays the\b  Enumeration Styles\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the format for displaying enumeration constants in the detail views for enumeration constants.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow16.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 16.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Specify enumerator styles for detail views of enumerator constants using the Enumeration Styles dialog.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Member_Filters_for_Classes}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Member Filters for Classes}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;class details, member filters;Browser, member filters for class details}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0039}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Member Filters for Classes \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \b  Member Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to specify the members you want to appear in the detail view of a class.  For example, you may not want the detail class to contain private members.  Alternatively, you may only wish to see function members and not data members.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Member Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Member Filters\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you specify the information you want to appear in detail views for classes.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow17.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 17.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Use the Member Filters dialog to select the information to appear in detail views for classes.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose the inheritance level\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Inherited Members\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog.
\par 
\par The options are:
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b None\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Do not show inherited members.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Visible\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Show the local members of a class and visible members of inherited classes.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b All\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Show all local and inherited members of a class.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the check boxes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Access Level\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog to select the desired access levels.
\par 
\par The options are Public, Protected, and Private.  Only members with the selected attributes will appear in the detail view for a class.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select the desired members\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Members\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  section of the dialog.
\par 
\par Show data members in a class by enabling the\b  variables\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check box.  When the\b  variables\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check box is enabled, you may also enable or disable static data members by clicking on the\b  static\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check box.
\par 
\par Show function members in a class by enabling the\b  functions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check box.  When the\b  functions\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check box is enabled, you may also enable or disable static and virtual function members by clicking on the\b  static\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  and\b  virtual\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  check boxes.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to accept the member filter query.
\par 
\par This closes the dialog and returns you to the active window.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The Default button on the Member Filter dialog resets the query to the default settings.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # AutoMarranging_of_Graph_Views}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Auto-arranging of Graph Views}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;graph views, auto-arranging;Browser, auto-arranging the graph view}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0040}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Auto-arranging of Graph Views \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par When collapsing a graph view, nodes that become hidden are replaced by gaps in the graph.\b   Arrange Graph\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Tree\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu compacts the graph view to remove the spaces left vacant by hidden nodes.
\par 
\par When enabled,\b  Graph Auto-arrange\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  of the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu causes the Browser to automatically compact the graph view each time you perform a collapse operation.  To enable the automatic compaction of the graph view after a collapse operation, select\b  Graph Auto-arrange\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  A check mark appears beside the menu item when it is enabled.  Select the menu item again to disable it.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Line_Drawing_Method_for_Graph_Views}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Line Drawing Method for Graph Views}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0041}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Line Drawing Method for Graph Views \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \b Graph Square Lines\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to select the type of line that connects the nodes in a graph view.  The default is to connect nodes of the graph using diagonal lines.  Choosing\b  Graph Square Lines\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu causes nodes to be connected using square lines (combinations of vertical and horizontal lines).  A check mark beside the menu item indicates this method of drawing lines is enabled.  To disable this option, select it again.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow18.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 18.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Graph Square Lines option changes the connecting lines from diagonal to square.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Changing_the_Graph_Orientation}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Changing the Graph Orientation}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, change graph orientation}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0042}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Changing the Graph Orientation \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par In the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu you can select whether the trees on the graph view grow horizontally or vertically.  A graph view that grows horizontally is one where the root node is at the left and the leaf nodes at the right.  A graph view that grows vertically is one where the root node is at the top and the leaf nodes at the bottom.  By default, graphs grow vertically.
\par 
\par Select\b  Graph Horizontal\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to change the graph orientation to horizontal.  Selecting this option changes the menu item name to\b  Graph Horizontal\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  Select this menu item to change the graph orientation back to vertical.
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24 \qc \{bmc brow19.bmp\}\f2 \fs20 
\par \ql \i \b Figure 19.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \i \b \b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  The Graph Horizontal option displays the graph with the root node at the left.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Defining_Graph_View_Legends}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Defining Graph View Legends}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, defining graph view legends;call legend menu item;inheritance legend menu item;graph view legends, in Browser}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0043}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Defining Graph View Legends \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par The\b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  and\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu items in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allow you to change the colors, line styles, and node styles used in the graph views.  Changing the styles updates all open graph views in the current session, as well as any new ones you open.  Saving the session configuration to an option file saves changes made to the colors and the lines and node styles.
\par 
\par The\b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  and\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialogs are designed differently, but the procedures for changing the graph styles are identical.
\par 
\par To change the line and node styles for inheritance graphs, choose\b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  The\b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog appears.
\par 
\par To change the line and node styles for call graphs, choose\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  The\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog appears.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the line or node style\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  or use the up, down, right and left arrow keys to select the line or node style you want to change.
\par 
\par A box appears around the selected line.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on the Modify button.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par This opens the\b  Draw Style\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog for the selected line or node style.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (3)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the desired line or node style\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b use the up and down arrow keys\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to select the desired line or node style.
\par 
\par The sample appearing in the\b  Example\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field at the top of the dialog now reflects the currently selected line or node style.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (4)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click once on the desired color or use the up and down\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  arrow keys to select the desired color.
\par 
\par The sample appearing in the\b  Example\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  field at the top of the dialog now reflects the currently selected color.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (5)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to accept the new style and color setting.
\par 
\par The\b  Draw Style\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog closes, returning you to the\b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  or\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Select another line or node style to change and repeat this procedure.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (6)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Click on OK in the\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0 \b  Inheritance Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  or\b  Call Legend\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog when you have changed all desired colors and styles for lines and nodes.
\par 
\par Clicking\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  closes the dialog and updates all open inheritance or call graph views with the selected colors and line and node styles.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Selecting_a_Text_Editor}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Selecting a Text Editor}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;set text editor;Browser, selecting a text editor}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0044}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Selecting a Text Editor \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Browser.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b To select your own text editor:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (1)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Choose Set Text Editor\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the Options menu.
\par 
\par The Set Text Editor dialog appears.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field.  You must also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL).  If the editor is an executable file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field.  The argument line will be supplied to the editor whenever it is started by the Browser.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will be filled in by the Browser.  The macros are:
\par 
\par \li720 \f2\fi-360 \b %f\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The name of the file to be edited.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b %r\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The row in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b %c\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 The column in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1.
\par \li360\fi0 
\par For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file='%f' row='%r' col='%c'\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the file\f4 \fs24  foobar.c\f2 \fs20  with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed to your editor is:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      file='foobar.c' row='215' col='31'\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position the text cursor.  If no row or column is available, then the Browser will supply the value of 1 as a default.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b (2)\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Select OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select Cancel\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor.
\par 
\par or
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b \plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b Select Default\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Automatically_Saving_Options_on_Exit}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Automatically Saving Options on Exit}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `Configuring_the_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, saving options on exit}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0045}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Automatically Saving Options on Exit \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par \b  Save Options on Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu instructs the Browser to automatically save the current options to an options file.  To enable the automatic saving of options, select\b  Save Options on Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu.  A check mark beside the menu item indicates that it is enabled.  To disable this option, select it again.
\par 
\par See the section entitled {\f2\uldb Saving\~Options\v\f2\uldb Saving_Options\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information on saving options to a file.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Loading_Options}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Loading Options}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;options, loading;Browser, loading options}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0046}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Loading Options \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par Use the\b  Load Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu to load an option file into your Browser session.
\par 
\par Choose\b  Load Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File menu.\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0   The\b  Load Options File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog appears where you select the option file you want to load for the current Browser session.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Saving_Options}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Saving Options}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;Browser, save options;Browser, saving options}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0047}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Saving Options \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par There are three ways to save the current options to an option file:
\par 
\par \li360 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Save Options
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Save Options As
\par \fi0 \fi-360\f1 \'b7\f2\tab Save Options on Exit
\par \fi0 \li0\fi0 
\par An asterisk beside the option file name in the caption bar indicates that changes were made to the options during the Browser session and should be saved.  This section describes each method for saving options to a file.
\par 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Note:\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 If you have made option changes during the Browser session and you choose\b  Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu without first saving the options, the Browser prompts you to save the options to a file.  Refer to the section entitled {\f2\uldb Quitting\~the\~Browser\v\f2\uldb Quitting_the_Browser\v0 \plain\f2\fs20} for more information.
\par \li0\fi0 
\par \li360 \f2\fi-360 \b Save Options\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 Selecting\b  Save Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  from the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu saves the updated options information to the current options file.  To save options in this way, an options file must be specified in the caption bar.  
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Save Options As\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Save Options As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  File\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu opens the\b  Save Options As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog.  Specify the filename of the option file to which you want to save the current options.  Click on\b  OK\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  to close the dialog and save the options.
\par 
\par \f2\fi-360 \b Save Options on Exit\plain\f2\fs20
\par \fi0 \b  Save Options on Exit\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in the\b  Options\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  menu allows you to specify that the Browser is to automatically save the current options to an options file, if one exists.  When enabled, a check mark appears beside this menu item.
\par 
\par When enabled and an option file exists, the Browser saves the changes without prompting when you close the session.
\par 
\par When enabled and no option file exists, the Browser displays a message box prompting you to save the new options.  Choose\b  No\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in this box to close the Browser session without saving.  Choose\b  Yes\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  in this box to display a\b  Save As\b0 \i0 \ul0 \ulw0  dialog where you specify the option file to which you want to save the new options.
\par \pard \page #{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 # Setting_Source_Search_Paths}${\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 $ Setting Source Search Paths}!{\footnote ! ChangeButtonBinding( "btn_up", "JumpId( `wbrw.hlp', `The_Open_Watcom_Browser')") }K{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 K ;path search;search path;source search path;Browser, path option;path option}+{\footnote \pard\plain \sl240 \fs20 + The Open Watcom Browser:0048}{\keepn \f2 \fs28 Setting Source Search Paths \f2 \fs20 \par \pard \f2 \fs20 \tx360 \tx720 \tx1080 \tx1440 \tx1800 \tx2160 \tx2520 \tx2880 \tx3240 \tx3600 \tx3960 \tx4320 \tx4680 \tx5040 \tx5400 }
\par You can specify a command line option to the Browser that allows you to specify alternate source file search paths.  This option is useful when a database file is created on a system different from the one that is used to browse the application source code.  The syntax of the command line option is:
\par 
\par \f4 \fs24       \f2 \fs20 
\par \f4 \fs24      path path_spec1;path_spec2;...\f2 \fs20 
\par 
\par Since the database files record explicit paths to source files, it is likely that the path will no longer be valid once the software is moved to another system.
\par 
\par When the Browser cannot locate the specified file using its explicit path, it will search the paths listed in the path option.
\par }
